40,2,1,talk,Minato,,"I found some things that make me a little sad. The coordination group is very small now, and two people are doing so much work by hand. Also, our developers are very busy until later this year.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings are equally sobering. I discovered that network coverage at customer plants is highly unstable, and getting device approval is a slow, arduous process. It seems the external environment is far more volatile than we assume.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,George,,"My findings are concerning. The capital budget is frozen, meaning we cannot afford new infrastructure. Furthermore, per-head licensing costs will only increase as we scale, making recurring expenses a significant long-term risk.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,May,,"My findings are a bit of a wake-up call! It turns out that big, ambitious projects requiring everyone's daily input just vanish, while small, specialized tools actually survive. Also, most ""standardization"" efforts just end up ownerless.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I also found some things that are quite worrying. Our teams are losing nearly an hour every day just copying records that already exist, and we've had several misrouted deliveries because different units have different addresses for the same firm.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Minato,,"@Midori, that sounds so tiring for the teams. It makes me worry that if we pick a big project, the people who are already working so hard by hand will have to do even more.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minato, your concern is well-placed. When we combine that with the fact that our developers are overstretched, any project requiring heavy manual input or complex setup risks becoming a burden rather than a benefit.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,George,,"@Jonathan, I agree. But we must also consider the financial burden. If we choose a project with per-head licensing, we are simply deferring a cost that will grow and haunt us in next year's budget.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,May,,"@George, you're always thinking about the bill! But look at the bigger picture: if we pick something too ""grand"" that requires everyone to participate, it'll just vanish like the others. We need something lean and specialized!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@May, I think you're right about keeping it lean. When I see people losing an hour a day to copying records, it feels like we should prioritize fixing those small, painful frictions over something grand that might just disappear.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I agree with Midori. It would be so kind to help the people who are tired. I am worried that if we don't think about who does the work, some people will be too sad and exhausted.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minato, your kindness is a guiding light. I believe we must also be mindful of the technical groundwork. If a project depends on unstable networks or slow approvals at customer sites, it will only lead to frustration and broken promises.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,George,,"@Jonathan, I agree that technical stability is key. However, we must be equally mindful of the financial structure. A project that seems lean now but relies on per-head licensing will eventually become an unsustainable drain on our resources.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,May,,"@George, you're such a buzzkill with the budget! But honestly, if we pick a project that's too broad, it won't even last long enough for those licenses to become a problem. Let's find something small and punchy that actually sticks!","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@May, I love your energy! But I think we can find a balance. If we pick a small, punchy project that specifically fixes those daily losses—like the record copying—we can be lean and truly helpful at the same time.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Minato,,"I think Midori has a very lovely idea. If we can find a project that is small and helps the tired people without needing the busy developers, that would be the most peaceful choice for everyone.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,I agree. A project that respects our current constraints—both human and technical—will bring the most peace. We must avoid any proposal that assumes a perfect environment or an infinite supply of labor.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,George,,"I concur. However, we must also ensure the project respects our financial constraints. A solution that avoids the frozen capital budget and avoids escalating per-head licenses is the only way to ensure it remains sustainable.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,May,,"You guys are practically writing the checklist for me! No big dreams, no budget nightmares, and definitely nothing that needs a crowd to work. Let's just find the one that's actually realistic!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I agree! Let's look for a candidate that solves those real, everyday frustrations without adding more stress to our colleagues' lives. I'm excited to see which proposal fits this balance best!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
